.TH HEAD 1.SH NAMEhead \- print the first lines of a file.SH SYNOPSIS\fBhead\fR[\-n lines] [\-c bytes] [\-b blocks] [\-qv] [file ...].br\fBhead\fR[\-#bcl] [\-qv] [file ...].SH DESCRIPTION\fBhead\fRis GNU head, from the textutils.  It copies the first ten lines of eachnamed file to the standard output, or of the standard input when no fileis named.  When more than one file is named, each is introduced by a line\fR`\fB==> name <==\fR'.\fR.SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-n\fI lines\fRthe first\fIlines\fRlines instead of ten.  The long form is\fB+lines=\fIn\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-c\fI bytes\fRthe first\fIbytes\fRbytes instead of lines.  Long form\fB+bytes=\fIn\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-b\fI blocks\fRthe first\fIblocks\fRblocks of 512 bytes.  Long form\fB+blocks=\fIn\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-#\fRa count written straight after the dash, optionally followed by aunit:\fBl\fRlines (the default),\fBc\fRbytes or\fBb\fRblocks.\fBhead -3\fRis three lines and\fBhead -5c\fRfive bytes..TP 12\fB\-q\fRnever print the\fB==>\fRheadings, even for several files.  Long forms\fB+quiet\fRand\fB+silent\fR.\fR.TP 12\fB\-v\fRalways print them, even for one file.  Long form\fB+verbose\fR.\fR.PPLong options begin with\fB+\fR,\fRnot\fB\-\-\fR:\fRthis build of the GNU tools predates the double dash..SH EXAMPLES.nfhead -n 3 notes         the first three lineshead -c 12 notes        the first twelve byteshead -v -n 1 a b        the first line of each, with headings.fi.SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fRWith the\fBcio\fRon this disk,\fBhead\fRalso writes a line naming each file it has read to the error output,after the lines themselves, and exits with status 1.  The output isright, and\fB2>/nil\fRhides the extra line.  Reading the standard input it does neither..SH SEE ALSOtail, tac, split